- UN reform and navigation. Called for a permanent Latin American and Caribbe=
an Council seat and more effective preventive diplomacy. Defended Canal neutr=
ality and equal passage, and warned that Hormuz disruption harms food, fertil=
izer, and energy supplies.
- Diplomacy and regional security. Supported negotiated ends to the wars in U=
kraine and the Middle East, free elections in the region, and cooperation aga=
inst drug trafficking and terrorism. Explicitly supported Colombia=E2=80=99s =
measures to reduce cocaine production.

PARAGUAY
Speaker: Santiago Pe=C3=B1a Palacios
Title: President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Western Hemisphere
- Venezuela and regional diplomacy. Defended opening dialogue with Venezuelan=
 authorities as support for a pluralistic transition, not endorsement of the =
government. Sought regional and UN help for Venezuela=E2=80=99s reintegration=
 and democratic institutions, and assistance to Nicaragua, Cuba, and Haiti.
- Taiwan and navigation. Reaffirmed support for Taiwan=E2=80=99s full and mea=
ningful participation in the UN and specialized agencies. Warned about Taiwan=
 Strait tensions and called for an end to attacks, reopened navigation throug=
h Hormuz and Bab al-Mandab, and negotiations.
- UN reform and leadership. Called for a more effective, representative Counc=
il and a Secretary-General from Latin America and the Caribbean with a strong=
er preventive-diplomacy role.
- Technology and values. Called for AI to serve human dignity, democratic acc=
ountability, freedom, and equality before the law. Reaffirmed protection of l=
ife from conception and the family; highlighted reported economic gains and r=
enewable electricity as a basis for investment.

POLAND
Speaker: Karol Nawrocki
Title: President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Europe & Eurasia
- Accountability and deterrence. Linked current insecurity to historical impu=
nity and condemned Russia=E2=80=99s invasion, hybrid operations, and threats =
to food security. Presented military modernization and NATO cooperation as de=
terrence, not preparation for aggression, and sought a coordinated response.
- Peacekeeping and Middle East. Called for stable mission funding and clearer=
 mandates covering civilian protection and institutional recovery. Supported =
de-escalation, a two-state solution, and Polish aid to Palestinian health and=
 employment programs and Lebanese civil defense.
- Economic engagement. Sought a Central European voice in the G20, noting par=
ticipation at the invitation of the U.S. presidency rather than established p=
ermanent membership. Promoted regional transport links, energy diversificatio=
n, nuclear power, and partnerships based on sovereignty.
- Social policy and technology. Advocated family support and opposition to ab=
ortion, rejected irregular migration as a demographic remedy, and raised pers=
ecution of Christians. Called for enforceable AI rules and environmental poli=
cies that preserve competitiveness rather than relocate emissions and industr=
y.

ROMANIA
Speaker: Nicu=C8=99or Dan
Title: President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Europe & Eurasia
- Ukraine and regional defense. Pledged continued support for Ukraine and Mol=
dova, citing refugee assistance, grain transit, Black Sea mine countermeasure=
s, a maritime-security hub with Bulgaria, and energy interconnections. Suppor=
ted a 5%-of-GDP defense-spending target and reported 3.7% for 2026.
- Middle East and navigation. Supported full implementation of President Trum=
p=E2=80=99s Gaza plan and the Board of Peace initiative, humanitarian evacuat=
ions, and Hamas disarmament. Called for a verifiable diplomatic agreement pre=
venting Iranian nuclear weapons and unrestricted navigation through Hormuz an=
d the Black Sea.
- Democracy and investment. Warned that coordinated fake social-media account=
s undermine individual expression and association. Promoted investment in Rom=
anian technology, manufacturing, energy, and critical resources, including pl=
anned Black Sea gas production beginning in 2027.
- UN and Francophonie engagement. Supported African-led peace efforts and pea=
cekeeping with clear mandates, measurable results, and responsible financing.=
 Sought support for Romania=E2=80=99s OIF Secretary-General candidate and hig=
hlighted participation in international initiatives for children=E2=80=99s ed=
ucation and safety.

RWANDA
Speaker: Justin Nsengiyumva
Title: Prime Minister
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Africa
Source note. Delivery on 23 September 2026 is corroborated by news reporting.=
 The supplied text=E2=80=99s 80th-session salutation remains inconsistent; it=
 is omitted from this summary. An official verbatim comparison remains outsta=
nding.
- Eastern DRC position. Described the FDLR as a long-standing security threat=
 and accused it of operating alongside Burundian forces and DRC-backed militi=
as and mercenaries. Argued that exclusion and governance failures require a p=
olitical solution; these are Rwanda=E2=80=99s stated claims.
- Diplomatic processes. Supported the Washington Accords and Doha process, th=
anked U.S., Qatari, and AU mediators, and urged the Security Council to suppo=
rt implementation fairly while addressing all parties=E2=80=99 stated concern=
s.
- UN reform. Called for UN80 to prioritize prevention of conflict and wider s=
hocks, inclusion, national development choices, and results rather than decla=
rations.

SERBIA
Speaker: Aleksandar Vu=C4=8Di=C4=87
Title: President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Europe & Eurasia
- Kosovo and international law. Reaffirmed Serbia=E2=80=99s territorial claim=
 and reliance on Resolution 1244, citing NATO=E2=80=99s bombing and Kosovo re=
cognition as examples of selective application of international law. Criticiz=
ed non-implementation of the Community of Serbian Municipalities under the Br=
ussels Agreement.
- External partnerships. Kept EU membership as a strategic priority while pro=
posing Western Balkan access to the single market and Schengen before accessi=
on. Expressed support for the Trump administration=E2=80=99s approach to Serb=
ia and maintained partnerships with China, Russia, and the Global South.
- UN reform. Supported a more effective UN, greater developing-country and Af=
rican Council representation, and another Eastern European Group seat. Said i=
nstitutional changes must be accompanied by consistent respect for sovereignt=
y and limits on force.
- AI and international engagement. Cited Serbia=E2=80=99s 2025 GPAI chairmans=
hip and the Belgrade Ministerial Declaration in support of safe, sustainable,=
 human-centered AI, accountability, and narrowing digital divides. Invited pa=
rticipation in Expo 2027 in Belgrade.

SLOVAKIA
Speaker: Peter Pellegrini
Title: President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Europe & Eurasia
- Council candidacy and reform. Sought support for Slovakia=E2=80=99s 2028=E2=
=80=932029 Council bid, with priorities covering international law, accountab=
le security institutions, strategic resources and infrastructure, and digital=
/AI security. Supported UN80, the Pact for the Future, and greater representa=
tion for underrepresented regions.
- AI and autonomous weapons. Called for equitable AI access and strong intern=
ational frameworks for autonomous weapons, with the UN in a leading role. Ins=
isted that machines should not independently make life-or-death decisions.
- Prevention and diplomacy. Supported responsible AI use for mediation, analy=
sis, and early warning under human oversight. Rejected changing borders by fo=
rce and backed serious diplomatic efforts to end Russia=E2=80=99s war against=
 Ukraine.
- Humanitarian priorities and leadership. Called for aid access and civilian =
protection in Gaza, a negotiated two-state solution, and attention to Sudan. =
Supported a transparent, inclusive Secretary-General selection based on merit=
, regional diversity, and gender balance.

SPAIN
Speaker: Pedro S=C3=A1nchez
Title: President of the Government
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Europe & Eurasia
- UN reform and technology. Called for equitable Council representation and a=
bolition of the permanent-member veto, stronger UN bodies and the ICC, renewe=
d nuclear non-proliferation and disarmament, and strict regulation of compani=
es controlling social networks and AI.
- Secretary-General selection. Explicitly called for a woman from Latin Ameri=
ca as the next Secretary-General, linking the appointment to regional represe=
ntation, gender equality, and stronger multilateral leadership.
- Conflict and military policy. Reaffirmed opposition to wars of aggression a=
nd refused a 5%-of-GDP military-spending target. Cited sanctions on Russia, a=
n arms embargo on Israel=E2=80=99s government, and restrictions on use of Spa=
nish territory for attacks; accused Israel=E2=80=99s government of genocide i=
n Gaza.
- Aid and migration. Reported increased development and multilateral contribu=
tions despite wider aid cuts. Reaffirmed the SDGs and said the response to mi=
gration pressures in Ceuta should uphold law and human rights rather than por=
tray migrants as enemies.

SYRIA
Speaker: Ahmad al-Sharaa
Title: President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Near East
- Recovery and sanctions. Presented Syria as moving from conflict toward reco=
nstruction and renewed diplomacy. Said the Caesar Act had been repealed, sanc=
tions lifted, and Syria removed from the state-sponsors-of-terrorism list.
- Returns and security. Reported the return of 3.5 million refugees and inter=
nally displaced people, promoted a =E2=80=9CSyria Without Refugee Camps=E2=80=
=9D initiative, and described action against Captagon and organized crime. Hi=
ghlighted cooperation with the OPCW, IAEA, and partner states on weapons-rela=
ted obligations.
- Israel and Golan. Reaffirmed Syria=E2=80=99s claim to the Golan under Secur=
ity Council resolution 497 and commitment to the 1974 agreement. Condemned Is=
raeli strikes and incursions and demanded withdrawal to the lines of 8 Decemb=
er 2024.

TIMOR-LESTE
Speaker: Jos=C3=A9 Ramos-Horta
Title: President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: East Asia & Pacific
- Nuclear initiative. Urged President Trump to convene nuclear-armed states, =
major military powers, and defense industries to establish no-first-strike co=
mmitments, regular communications, and a decade-long path toward reducing and=
 eliminating nuclear arsenals.
- Regional diplomacy. Condemned Iran=E2=80=99s attacks on neighboring states,=
 welcomed U.S. humanitarian assistance to Cuba, and supported a two-state sol=
ution. Described Morocco=E2=80=99s Western Sahara proposal as an opportunity =
for a negotiated settlement that consults the people directly concerned.
- UN reform. Called for a woman from Latin America as the next Secretary-Gene=
ral, expansion of both Security Council membership categories, and reform of =
the veto. Said efficiency measures must preserve essential support to develop=
ing countries.
- Development and inclusion. Sought predictable climate finance, technology t=
ransfer, and developing-country participation in AI governance. Reaffirmed AS=
EAN centrality and balanced relations with major powers while urging greater =
attention to neglected humanitarian crises.

UGANDA
Speaker: Jessica Rose Epel Alupo
Title: Vice President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Africa
- UN leadership and representation. Presented Ambassador Olara Otunnu as Ugan=
da=E2=80=99s candidate for Secretary-General. Called for African permanent an=
d non-permanent Council representation and supported efficiency reforms that =
do not dilute agreed mandates or the 2030 Agenda.
- International finance and taxation. Sought financial-system reform and grea=
ter developing-country voice. Supported negotiations on the UN Framework Conv=
ention on International Tax Cooperation, including protocols on cross-border =
services and tax dispute resolution, to address illicit flows and profit shif=
ting.
- Development and NAM. Advocated a binding international covenant on the righ=
t to development and announced a NAM ministerial meeting in Kampala for 2=E2=
=80=934 December 2026. Described national priorities in agro-industry, touris=
m, minerals, science, and technology as the basis for its growth goals.
- Peace and health. Called for locally owned political solutions and regional=
 cooperation rather than military responses alone. Sought financing for healt=
h systems, pandemic preparedness, and equitable access to diagnostics, medici=
nes, and vaccines.

UKRAINE
Speaker: Volodymyr Zelenskyy
Title: President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Europe & Eurasia
- Pressure on Russia. Urged continued sanctions, reduced trade with Russia, d=
efense assistance, and joint production to limit Russia=E2=80=99s war financi=
ng. Described Ukrainian strikes as directed at the resources sustaining the w=
ar; battlefield and economic assessments were the speaker=E2=80=99s claims.
- Defense partnerships. Invited countries to join the FREIA anti-ballistic pr=
ogram as a lower-cost, scalable complement=E2=80=94not replacement=E2=80=94fo=
r Patriot and SAMP-T systems. Promoted integrated drone-defense partnerships =
for infrastructure and borders, reporting new agreements with Finland and Aus=
tralia.
- Winter and AI risks. Called for diplomacy backed by strength and de-escalat=
ion before winter to protect energy infrastructure. Sought greater Chinese in=
fluence on Russia and warned that expanding AI-enabled battlefield decision-m=
aking adds urgency to ending the war.
- Foreign recruitment. Said Russia was using citizens of 47 countries in the =
war and urged affected governments to protect their nationals from recruitmen=
t or deception. Also warned of wider security consequences from Russian=E2=80=
=93North Korean military cooperation.

UZBEKISTAN
Speaker: Saida Mirziyoyeva
Title: Head of the Presidential Administration
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: South & Central Asia
- UN engagement. Supported UN80 and Council expansion, sought support for Uzb=
ekistan=E2=80=99s 2035=E2=80=932036 non-permanent Council bid, and said it wo=
uld assume the NAM chairmanship in 2027 to promote dialogue.
- Regional diplomacy and human capital. Highlighted improved Central Asian co=
operation and called for constructive engagement with Afghanistan rather than=
 isolation. Announced a forthcoming UNFPA dialogue on demographic stability a=
nd human capital and an October gathering in Samarkand on children=E2=80=99s =
well-being.
- Water and energy. Promoted Aral Sea restoration, water-saving technologies,=
 afforestation, and a target of 54% renewable energy by 2030. Urged practical=
 implementation of the announced 2027=E2=80=932036 afforestation and reforest=
ation decade.
- AI and legal reform. Called for AI governance rooted in human rights, secur=
ity, equal opportunity, and accountability. Reported an English-law-based com=
mercial court initiative, action against child and forced labor and domestic =
violence, support for women=E2=80=99s enterprise, and interreligious dialogue.

VENEZUELA
Speaker: Delcy Rodr=C3=ADguez
Title: Acting President
Date: 23 SEP 2026
Region: Western Hemisphere
- Political transition. Announced what she described as an orderly, instituti=
onal, verifiable transition, including judicial reform, opposition dialogue, =
and elections with equal participation. The speech gave no election date and =
left timing to Venezuelan processes; implementation is not established by the=
 announcement.
- United States and recovery. Thanked President Trump for renewed diplomatic =
engagement and highlighted a recent U.S.=E2=80=93Venezuelan energy agreement.=
 Sought cooperation on energy, security, transnational crime, and economic de=
velopment, and thanked partners for assistance after the reported 24 June ear=
thquake.
- Essequibo and sovereignty. Reasserted Venezuela=E2=80=99s claim over the Es=
sequibo territory and called on Guyana to return to bilateral negotiations un=
der the Geneva Agreement. Requested international support that respects Venez=
uela=E2=80=99s self-determination and opposed external interference.
- Energy, development, and AI. Opposed sanctions and imposed decarbonization =
timelines, arguing for a balance of supply security, affordability, and susta=
inability. Called for AI to benefit citizens, support development, and avoid =
widening digital inequality.

